  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Pygocentrus nattereri</em> occurs in the Amazon River basin, Paraguay-Paraná River basin, north-eastern Brazilian  coastal rivers and Essequibo River basin, in Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil,  Colombia, Ecuador, Guyana, Paraguay, Peru, Suriname, French Guiana, Venezuela and Uruguay (Fricke <em>et al</em>. 2020). In addition, this species is also present outside its natural range as an invasive species in the United States and Hawaiian Islands.",
    "population": "<p>There are no data on population size and trends for this species, but it is very common and abundant and is considered to be stable.</p>",
    "habitats": "<span>This species is common in creeks and interconnected ponds in Mato Grosso, Brazil,  where it influences distribution and feeding of other fish (Sazima and Machado 1990)  and in areas of high primary productivity in Rio Machado and Rio Negro.&#160;Medium-sized to large individuals (15-24 cm length) forage mainly at dawn, late afternoon and night up to about 10 pm, whereas smaller fish (8-11 cm) are active mainly during the day (Sazima and Machado 1990).<span>&#160;It feeds on insects,  worms and fish (Mills and Vevers 1989). <span><span>Tooth replacement on alternating sides of jaw allows continuous  feeding.  Its powerful dentition  can inflict serious bites. It has a  highly evolved auditory capacity and  a 'lurking', then 'dashing'  behaviour during daytime.</span></span>",
    "threats": "<p>There are no apparent significant threats to this species. However, since this is  a migratory species, dam construction and pollution is affecting the species marginally.</p>",
    "measures": "<p>This species does not have any conservation measures in place.</p>",
    "use_trade": "The species is harvested for food by local communities and for the aquarium trade. In Brazil the species is used in ornamental trade (IBAMA 2012).",
    "rationale": "<p>The species is widely distributed in South America with many  occurrences, especially in Brazil. There is no information about any  major threats to it in the countries where it occurs. Therefore, it is  assessed as Least Concern.</p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
